1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They're not your standard pickups.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Vehicles with cameras, cranes, and computer screens.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Arms, afterburners, and attitude.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
All in trash or just plain hauling.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
So grab a drink, take a load off, stretch out,

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
and get fired up for Extreme Trucks.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,560
There was a time when a truck was a truck.

9
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:41,900
Man's best mechanical friend.

10
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:45,400
A pal to help carry the burden.

11
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:48,340
Or give you a lift when you're feeling down.

12
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:52,280
Those vehicles could do everything.

13
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:53,880
Or so we thought.

14
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:58,200
There's a new breed of extreme trucks.

15
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:00,120
And they know plenty of tricks.

16
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,680
They can roll over, play fetch, even sniff out criminals.

17
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:06,220
And they're begging for some attention.

18
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:08,840
Like this one.

19
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:17,360
Its body is made of high-tensile strength steel, similar to armor plating.

20
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:25,960
Its high-tech electronic equipment includes exterior cameras and video monitors.

21
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,720
Its movements are tracked by GPS.

22
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:36,540
And it even sports a robotic arm with joystick control.

23
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:40,500
No, it's not a James Bond car.

24
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:45,000
It's the Auto Reach by McNeilis Incorporated.

25
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,160
An extreme garbage truck.

26
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:53,040
A vehicle designed for your typical stop and throw traffic.

27
00:01:53,041 --> 00:02:02,440
On the average, an automated collection truck like this will pick up anywhere between 1,000 and 1,500 homes a day.

28
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:08,300
The average manual collection truck will only be able to average 300 to 400 homes a day.

29
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:17,840
The muscle behind the machine's efficiency is its articulated arm.

30
00:02:17,841 --> 00:02:27,500
it can reach out nine and a half feet, grab the garbage, and curl a can weighing up to 2,000 pounds.

31
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:34,400
Try curling 2,000 pounds 1,500 times in a day.

32
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:37,300
This arm never gets tired.

33
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:40,240
Neither does its operator.

34
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:46,280
Rear-loading garbage trucks used to require guys riding on the back to throw refuse in the rear.

35
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:51,200
Now the articulated arm does all the heavy lifting.

36
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,820
It also has an 8-foot side-to-side swing,

37
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,360
allowing it to reach around obstacles,

38
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:59,080
like a mailbox,

39
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,960
or the car of the space cadet who parks on the street during collection day.

40
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,200
The auto reach is manufactured in Dodge Center, Minnesota,

41
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:10,840
at the McNeilis factory.

42
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:15,820
And what we're doing in this area is attaching the arm itself to the chassis.

43
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:19,020
This thing is all run hydraulically.

44
00:03:19,021 --> 00:03:23,000
The engine hooks up to the hydraulic pump which gives it the pressure to run through

45
00:03:23,001 --> 00:03:27,160
the valve and then the valve just diverts the flow to the different functions with the

46
00:03:27,161 --> 00:03:35,940
hydraulic cylinders to make the arm function.

47
00:03:35,941 --> 00:03:40,440
Inside the truck the operator has joystick control similar to the average video game

48
00:03:40,441 --> 00:03:43,620
controller so it's very simple for most people to learn.

49
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:46,740
When you move the joystick out, the arm goes out.

50
00:03:46,741 --> 00:03:49,220
When you move the joystick in, the arm comes in.

51
00:03:49,221 --> 00:03:51,980
Hold the joystick back, the arm comes up.

52
00:03:51,981 --> 00:03:54,180
Push it forward, the arm comes down.

53
00:03:54,181 --> 00:03:55,860
The toggle switch where my thumb moves

54
00:03:55,861 --> 00:03:57,500
opens and closes the grabber.

55
00:03:57,501 --> 00:03:59,380
When you're done, bring the arm in, tuck it,

56
00:03:59,381 --> 00:04:01,600
and drive to the next can.

57
00:04:01,601 --> 00:04:05,800
Our all-can grabber is designed so that it can pick up

58
00:04:05,801 --> 00:04:08,280
various can shapes and sizes.

59
00:04:08,281 --> 00:04:10,560
It's a fine line between crushing the can

60
00:04:10,561 --> 00:04:13,200
and grabbing it with enough force so that as it brings it up

61
00:04:13,201 --> 00:04:16,620
to unload the refuse, it doesn't drop it into the hopper.

62
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:23,160
It took almost 100 years for motorized garbage trucks to get this extreme.

63
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:30,120
The first garbage trucks were simply open dump trucks.

64
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:38,080
But by 1915, the first specially built covered body trucks were introduced in Europe

65
00:04:38,081 --> 00:04:42,220
in an effort to keep rodents out and odors in.

66
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:49,440
However, the early bodies were difficult to load, as garbage had to be lifted above shoulder

67
00:04:49,441 --> 00:04:53,040
level to dump.

68
00:04:53,041 --> 00:05:00,400
In 1929, refuse collectors got a helping hand when Heil built this collecto body, one of

69
00:05:00,401 --> 00:05:05,560
the first with an external hopper design.

70
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:10,200
The concept utilized a small hopper, which was more easily loaded because it was filled

71
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:12,200
at waste level.

72
00:05:12,201 --> 00:05:19,340
When full, a cable-driven lifting mechanism powered by the truck's power takeoff hoisted

73
00:05:19,341 --> 00:05:22,720
the hopper and dumped it into the top of the body.

74
00:05:22,721 --> 00:05:31,340
A door on top automatically opened and closed to keep odors from escaping.

75
00:05:31,341 --> 00:05:36,120
The Dempster Brothers later popularized a variation of the hopper design, the front-end

76
00:05:36,121 --> 00:05:37,120
loader.

77
00:05:37,121 --> 00:05:41,240
It used hydraulic arms to lift and dump.

78
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:47,340
a dumpster.

79
00:05:47,341 --> 00:05:52,240
In 1938, Garwood Industries put real pressure on the refuse business

80
00:05:52,241 --> 00:05:56,380
when they introduced their load packer body, believed to be the first truck with

81
00:05:56,381 --> 00:05:58,800
a hydraulic compactor.

82
00:05:58,801 --> 00:06:02,860
The rear loaded truck used hydraulic cylinders to force a pair of panels

83
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:06,880
forward, compacting the trash.

84
00:06:06,881 --> 00:06:10,660
The truck could hold roughly five tons of compacted garbage.

85
00:06:10,661 --> 00:06:13,560
a vast improvement which would revolutionize the industry.

86
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:27,760
The compactor on the auto reach makes those first vehicles seem like toys.

87
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:33,560
The body has a hopper that holds approximately five cubic yards of garbage.

88
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:39,440
In that hopper, we have a packing mechanism that has two hydraulically actuated cylinders.

89
00:06:39,441 --> 00:06:41,800
is once the body is filled with garbage,

90
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:44,140
the compaction starts to take place,

91
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:46,460
and the garbage is now packing against the other garbage,

92
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:50,080
thus creating the compaction ratio of 5 to 1.

93
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:54,300
The compactor crushes its pungent payload

94
00:06:54,301 --> 00:06:57,140
under 80,000 pounds per square inch of pressure.

95
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:03,880
The 28-cubic-yard body can hold about 12 tons of compacted garbage.

96
00:07:03,881 --> 00:07:10,980
The inside of the garbage truck is one of the worst environments we've ever seen.

97
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:15,620
You've got all the material that's packed inside the body under some pretty extreme

98
00:07:15,621 --> 00:07:16,321
pressures.

99
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:22,260
High tensile strength steel is used to make the truck tougher than the materials going

100
00:07:22,261 --> 00:07:22,961
into it.

101
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:27,240
What we've got coming in here is different widths of the steel, different grades of the

102
00:07:27,241 --> 00:07:27,941
steel.

103
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:33,860
Anything from a regular grade 50 material, which is a 50,000 tensile steel, up to 50,000

104
00:07:33,861 --> 00:07:37,460
to like this material that you see here is 190,000 pounds.

105
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:41,960
Similar to like an armor plate, it's a very, very tough, durable steel

106
00:07:41,961 --> 00:07:43,860
that we use in the size of our garbage trucks.

107
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:01,720
As the body is being built, the electronics are also tested,

108
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:03,440
including the video system.

109
00:08:03,860 --> 00:08:10,380
I'm in the back of the truck standing in front of a camera that's showing you the image of me in the cab of the chassis.

110
00:08:10,381 --> 00:08:14,340
From there, the operator of this body can see exactly what's behind them.

111
00:08:15,460 --> 00:08:19,280
And headquarters can see where the truck is thanks to GPS transmitters.

112
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:31,620
Oshkosh, parent company of McNeilis, is working on a hybrid diesel-electric refuse truck,

113
00:08:31,621 --> 00:08:35,820
which is expected to get 40% better gas mileage than current models.

114
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:42,980
With 136,000 garbage trucks on the road today,

115
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:46,300
traveling 25,000 miles a year on average,

116
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:51,260
it could mean a savings of 470 million gallons of fuel a year.

117
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:56,980
Oh, they'll also be a lot quieter.

118
00:08:57,720 --> 00:08:59,500
Great for those early morning pickings.

119
00:08:59,501 --> 00:09:05,360
reduced emissions, better gas mileage, quieter operation.

120
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:08,540
It'll be a real slam dunk.

121
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:19,620
Over 40% of garbage trucks on the road today are more than 10 years old

122
00:09:19,621 --> 00:09:22,520
and get less than three miles to the gallon.

123
00:09:23,380 --> 00:09:25,720
Extreme Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

124
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:43,500
Think of this one extreme vehicle as the Swiss Army knife of trucks.

125
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:47,980
It's one of the most versatile vehicles in the world,

126
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:53,420
combining the implement attachments of a tractor and the speed and comfort of a truck.

127
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:57,020
It's the German-engineered Unimog.

128
00:09:57,021 --> 00:10:04,020
Unimog is a contraction of the German universal motor garage, which means universal motor tool.

129
00:10:04,021 --> 00:10:09,020
It's the name that's been in place since the vehicle was first put into production in the early 1950s.

130
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:17,020
The Unimog is a four-wheel drive vehicle with dozens of attachments, which simply bolt on.

131
00:10:17,021 --> 00:10:26,660
The same vehicle can be converted from a mower, to a street sweeper, to a tree trimmer, to

132
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:32,420
a snow blower in mere minutes.

133
00:10:32,421 --> 00:10:37,000
Although new to North America, the Unimogs have been gaining popularity in other countries

134
00:10:37,001 --> 00:10:45,740
for over 50 years in both civilian and military applications.

135
00:10:45,741 --> 00:10:48,300
The Unimog has a colorful history.

136
00:10:48,301 --> 00:10:53,600
The first sketches of the early Unimogs date back to December of 1945.

137
00:10:53,601 --> 00:10:57,620
And the gentleman credited with the concept and the idea of a highly mobile implement

138
00:10:57,621 --> 00:11:02,500
carrier is a gentleman named Albert Friedrich, who was an aerospace engineer for Damler-Benz

139
00:11:02,501 --> 00:11:03,500
at the time.

140
00:11:03,501 --> 00:11:07,500
And he saw the need to feed the population in post-war Europe.

141
00:11:07,501 --> 00:11:13,440
They needed equipment to plow the fields, harvest the crops, and take care of all the

142
00:11:13,441 --> 00:11:16,560
jobs and necessities in feeding the population.

143
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:24,160
But before the Unimog could be put into production, Friedrich had to convince the occupying forces

144
00:11:24,161 --> 00:11:27,560
the four-wheel drive truck wouldn't be used as a military vehicle.

145
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:35,780
After much debate, he was granted a manufacturing permit in November 1945.

146
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:42,380
In less than a year, engineers took the rough sketches of a fellow engineer

147
00:11:42,381 --> 00:11:44,840
and built the first Unimog chassis.

148
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:51,060
Even the early models of this unique hybrid truck and tractor

149
00:11:51,061 --> 00:11:52,720
had implement attachment points,

150
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:55,700
as well as power takeoffs to run equipment.

151
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,760
German farmers loved the multifunctional vehicle.

152
00:12:01,220 --> 00:12:04,140
They would do everything from plowing the fields to harvesting vegetables

153
00:12:04,141 --> 00:12:06,000
to running all the implements farmers run,

154
00:12:06,001 --> 00:12:08,460
and then on Sundays they could take the vehicle to church.

155
00:12:08,461 --> 00:12:13,460
Even the wheel spacing was designed with farmers in mind.

156
00:12:13,461 --> 00:12:19,460
The width between wheels corresponded to the space between two rows of potatoes.

157
00:12:19,461 --> 00:12:23,460
But farmers weren't the only ones interested.

158
00:12:23,461 --> 00:12:28,460
The Unimog became popular for fighting fires,

159
00:12:28,461 --> 00:12:31,460
road maintenance,

160
00:12:31,461 --> 00:12:35,460
moving snow,

161
00:12:35,461 --> 00:12:36,340
Moving lumber.

162
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:39,640
Or moving whatever.

163
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:47,800
Large-scale production began in 1950 at the Daimler Benz plant in Germany.

164
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:55,900
Since then, the company sold 320,000 vehicles in 180 countries.

165
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:04,600
Some of Daimler Chrysler's biggest clients are municipalities that have different seasonal needs.

166
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:11,500
Instead of buying several special purpose machines, they can buy an all-in-one tool.

167
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:18,760
Because the Unimog has been around so long, there's plenty of implements available.

168
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:27,680
This front-mounted mowing platform senses obstacles and automatically mows around them.

169
00:13:27,681 --> 00:13:36,360
The articulated boom has a 23-foot reach.

170
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:42,360
At the end of the day, the boom folds up neatly and is ready for highway travel.

171
00:13:45,940 --> 00:13:49,520
In the winter, simply snap on a snowplow attachment.

172
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:57,100
Here we have a U-500 with a Schmidt snowblower.

173
00:13:57,580 --> 00:14:00,940
This particular implement is using two of the Unimog's output power options.

174
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:06,600
First, we're driving the main rotor via shaft from the output PTO in the front of the engine.

175
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:13,460
Secondly, we can control all the movements to the snowblower with an onboard hydraulic system.

176
00:14:13,461 --> 00:14:16,440
This controls the left, right, and up and down on the snowblower.

177
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:22,880
Like a tractor, the Unimog has a power takeoff, or PTO.

178
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:34,860
which transfers power from the engine to the implement via a spinning shaft.

179
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:41,520
The Unimog can have up to three PTOs.

180
00:14:43,300 --> 00:14:48,140
Also like a tractor, the Unimog has a hydraulic system to operate backhoes,

181
00:14:49,420 --> 00:14:50,340
front-end loaders,

182
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:52,600
manlifts,

183
00:14:56,340 --> 00:14:57,040
cranes,

184
00:14:57,100 --> 00:15:00,600
and other hydraulic equipment.

185
00:15:00,601 --> 00:15:03,600
Both hydraulic and PTO driven implements

186
00:15:03,601 --> 00:15:10,600
are made for quick attach detach.

187
00:15:10,601 --> 00:15:15,600
But unlike a tractor, the Unimog can zip to a site at 70 miles per hour,

188
00:15:15,601 --> 00:15:18,600
then go off-road if necessary.

189
00:15:18,601 --> 00:15:22,600
One of the strengths of the Unimog is its ability to get into very difficult terrain.

190
00:15:22,601 --> 00:15:25,600
The Unimog has a minimum 18 inches of ground clearance,

191
00:15:25,601 --> 00:15:27,440
so you can go it over obstacles.

192
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:30,480
It's also equipped with full-time four-wheel drive

193
00:15:30,481 --> 00:15:33,460
with both axle and interaxle differential locks

194
00:15:33,461 --> 00:15:35,340
so you can get power to all four wheels.

195
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:39,060
The vehicle's capable of climbing better than a 70% grade.

196
00:15:39,540 --> 00:15:41,520
It'll take a 38-degree side slope,

197
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:43,540
which, if you think about 38 degrees,

198
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:46,420
you're kind of in the vehicle at a pretty severe angle.

199
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:48,600
The truck has a very low center of gravity.

200
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:50,540
The engine is mounted low in the frame.

201
00:15:51,180 --> 00:15:53,240
Transmission is remote-mounted also very low.

202
00:15:55,600 --> 00:15:58,420
Driving the Unimog is actually quite simple.

203
00:15:58,600 --> 00:16:00,280
It's much like any other vehicle.

204
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:03,860
You have a three-pedal system where you have a clutch, gas, and a brake.

205
00:16:05,380 --> 00:16:09,440
Range speed varies from 240 feet per hour to 70 miles an hour.

206
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:11,900
It's all how you have it geared.

207
00:16:12,220 --> 00:16:14,140
You can have different gear options from the factory.

208
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:21,440
And in case you want to take your Unimog for a spin in England...

209
00:16:21,441 --> 00:16:25,980
VarioPilot allows the driver to operate the vehicle from either the right side or the

210
00:16:25,981 --> 00:16:29,720
left hand side of the vehicle.

211
00:16:29,721 --> 00:16:37,940
Simply remove the access panel.

212
00:16:37,941 --> 00:16:42,920
Push the release button and lift the latch which brings the pedals off the floor.

213
00:16:42,921 --> 00:16:46,320
Slide the steering wheel over and latch it back down.

214
00:16:46,321 --> 00:16:47,820
Set out this panel and you're ready to go again.

215
00:16:56,080 --> 00:17:07,520
With the ability to mow, lift, push, pull, cut, spray, sweep, grind, haul, and scoop,

216
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:11,160
there's not many things the Unimog and its implements can't do.

217
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:16,160
The only thing this Swiss Army knife of trucks is missing is a corkscrew.

218
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:20,700
Okay, it's got everything.

219
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:29,960
10.06 a.m., Baltimore, Maryland.

220
00:17:31,360 --> 00:17:37,300
The Baltimore Organized Crime Division Technical Assistance Response Unit is called into action.

221
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:44,580
They race to the scene in their own extreme truck.

222
00:17:44,581 --> 00:17:47,720
a state-of-the-art mobile command center.

223
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:53,900
Truck's going out to a barricade for a person who run into a house,

224
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:56,480
possibility of a hostage inside.

225
00:17:56,481 --> 00:18:01,760
We're going to assist TAC as an off-site command post by having the truck there.

226
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:09,160
They don't have to go to other locations for faxes or video or lighting, camera, anything of that nature.

227
00:18:09,161 --> 00:18:16,300
the truck arrives minutes later as does the quick response team of the tactical unit

228
00:18:16,301 --> 00:18:30,301
this incident is a home break-in gone bad the perpetrator was interrupted when a woman and her

229
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:36,760
kids came home and surprised him now he's taken them hostage and it's up to the negotiator to

230
00:18:36,761 --> 00:18:38,280
try to talk him into giving them up.

231
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:39,920
It's about two doors in.

232
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:41,260
Just look at that open door.

233
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:43,320
You see the SWAT guys right there.

234
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:45,880
Routinely, we get called out on these situations

235
00:18:45,881 --> 00:18:47,320
for hostage negotiations.

236
00:18:48,580 --> 00:18:52,400
Also to provide perimeter video surveillance

237
00:18:52,401 --> 00:18:54,920
through the helicopter that's flying above us now.

238
00:18:56,020 --> 00:18:58,980
In less than 10 minutes, the truck's up and running.

239
00:19:02,820 --> 00:19:04,680
Detective Craig deploys the truck's

240
00:19:04,681 --> 00:19:09,129
two telescoping communication masts.

241
00:19:09,130 --> 00:19:15,680
One that can extend to 58 feet, the other to 52 feet,

242
00:19:15,681 --> 00:19:20,553
can transmit and receive microwave signals.

243
00:19:20,554 --> 00:19:25,540
On top are two remote video cameras, peering

244
00:19:25,541 --> 00:19:27,380
over the building which is providing cover.

245
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:33,560
And relay the helicopter

246
00:19:33,561 --> 00:19:35,680
footage or truck footage back to police

247
00:19:35,681 --> 00:19:36,381
headquarters.

248
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:39,720
Thanks to two 10,000 watt electric

249
00:19:39,721 --> 00:19:41,560
generators, the truck is

250
00:19:41,561 --> 00:19:42,820
completely self-sufficient.

251
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:45,420
This extreme truck is basically a

252
00:19:45,421 --> 00:19:47,560
portable police station slash

253
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:48,360
TV station.

254
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:51,580
While the tactical

255
00:19:51,581 --> 00:19:53,160
team prepares to enter the building,

256
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:56,060
the negotiator continues to talk with the suspect.

257
00:19:57,880 --> 00:20:00,660
It's been several hours since the situation began.

258
00:20:01,820 --> 00:20:06,040
If it drags into the night, the mobile command center will be ready.

259
00:20:08,060 --> 00:20:12,440
Another important feature to this truck is we have what we call stadium lighting.

260
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:15,420
The arm has four very high-powered halogen lights.

261
00:20:15,421 --> 00:20:17,720
The arm will raise up and turn.

262
00:20:18,060 --> 00:20:21,800
So if we want to light this whole block up, we just raise it and fire it up.

263
00:20:23,160 --> 00:20:24,920
Suddenly, some movement.

264
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:27,720
Tactical moves in.

265
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:36,840
Finally, someone exits the building.

266
00:20:40,880 --> 00:20:42,620
Today, no one gets hurt.

267
00:20:43,140 --> 00:20:46,820
The hostage negotiators were able to talk the perpetrator out.

268
00:20:46,821 --> 00:20:49,440
He released the two children and the woman first,

269
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:52,260
and then he came out, was handcuffed and taken away.

270
00:20:54,160 --> 00:20:58,140
This was just another day for the Baltimore Technical Assistance Response Unit.

271
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:02,860
The truck is deployed an average of four times a week.

272
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:09,720
Trucks like this are the latest high-tech tools for homeland defense.

273
00:21:10,820 --> 00:21:15,240
Narcotic raids, hostage situations, and event security.

274
00:21:15,241 --> 00:21:23,560
This is the Baltimore City Police Mobile Command Center.

275
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:28,740
In this area, this is where we do our surveillance and radio communications.

276
00:21:29,040 --> 00:21:32,160
With this, you can control the cameras on top of the truck.

277
00:21:32,300 --> 00:21:35,120
You can zoom in and out, get different angles of the picture.

278
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:38,520
And he's controlling that basically by the mouse of the computer.

279
00:21:38,521 --> 00:21:45,400
The police also have cameras on various buildings and several street corners throughout Baltimore.

280
00:21:46,780 --> 00:21:50,400
The truck can control those cameras and download the feed.

281
00:21:50,820 --> 00:21:57,160
So we can sit here at this location and watch what's going on across town or a couple miles away.

282
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:03,600
This truck is loaded with electronics, satellite TV,

283
00:22:03,601 --> 00:22:08,740
multiple landline cell and satellite phones

284
00:22:08,741 --> 00:22:11,920
and eight TV monitors, among other things.

285
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:18,180
Right here is the conference room of the mobile command truck.

286
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:22,200
The video that we're watching up front we can also put into this screen,

287
00:22:22,201 --> 00:22:24,020
which is a 42-inch plasma TV.

288
00:22:26,040 --> 00:22:30,200
This extreme command truck is equipped with the latest communication technology.

289
00:22:30,201 --> 00:22:34,740
But its roots date back more than 75 years.

290
00:22:37,040 --> 00:22:41,240
Understanding a quicker response time equaled more criminals apprehended.

291
00:22:43,080 --> 00:22:49,080
The Detroit police installed the nation's first one-way voice-based car radios in 1928.

292
00:22:51,280 --> 00:22:56,700
A car could receive a call, but had to pull over and telephone a response back to headquarters.

293
00:22:56,701 --> 00:23:02,640
Despite the inconvenience, it dramatically improved efficiency.

294
00:23:04,500 --> 00:23:09,240
Then in 1933, officers got the ability to talk back

295
00:23:09,241 --> 00:23:14,740
when the Bayonne, New Jersey Police Department installed the first two-way police car radios.

296
00:23:14,780 --> 00:23:17,240
Police headquarters Bayonne, calling all cars.

297
00:23:17,940 --> 00:23:19,080
Okay, number three.

298
00:23:19,160 --> 00:23:22,080
The system was credited for one arrest in every ten calls.

299
00:23:22,081 --> 00:23:27,780
The obvious benefits prompted other departments to install radio equipment in their cars.

300
00:23:29,860 --> 00:23:35,980
Throughout the 20th century, police departments have looked for better ways of collecting and disseminating information.

301
00:23:36,580 --> 00:23:41,600
Mobile command centers have evolved out of necessity over about the last 20, 30 years.

302
00:23:42,220 --> 00:23:48,700
Sometime back, law enforcement agencies started operating out of suburbans or the backs of pickup trucks,

303
00:23:48,701 --> 00:23:51,020
and they had some radio equipment there,

304
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:55,860
and that brought some centralization to a specific scene or an incident.

305
00:23:56,960 --> 00:24:02,420
In the last 15 years, people began to build these mobile command centers from the ground up.

306
00:24:05,320 --> 00:24:10,146
This mobile command center is the product of the Allied Defense Group and LDV, Inc.

307
00:24:10,147 --> 00:24:11,600
of Burlington, Wisconsin.

308
00:24:11,601 --> 00:24:19,360
manufacturer of SWAT, Bomb Squad, Hazmat, Crime Scene Investigation, and other specialty trucks.

309
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:24,900
LDV starts with an empty shell when building a mobile command center.

310
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:28,720
At the metal fabrication shop, we cut the holes in the body.

311
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:33,660
We install the compartments, the observation decks, any kind of catwalk.

312
00:24:33,661 --> 00:24:38,880
After that, we'll install the generator and the leveling systems on the vehicle.

313
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:45,680
Next comes the wiring.

314
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,900
We literally put miles of wiring for this truck,

315
00:24:50,280 --> 00:24:53,060
and it's all the support of the systems that are integrated into it,

316
00:24:53,420 --> 00:24:56,140
and it can either be 12 volts, it can be 110 volt AC,

317
00:24:56,760 --> 00:24:58,800
it can be computer network or phone cable.

318
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:06,420
LDV installs custom-built cabinets to house electronics.

319
00:25:06,421 --> 00:25:11,960
This particular cabinet is what's called the communication cabinet.

320
00:25:12,620 --> 00:25:19,480
Inside here you see the cell phones, you see the modular switching system similar to a small business PBX,

321
00:25:19,880 --> 00:25:25,640
the amplifier for the external PA system, and the computer network is all routed right here.

322
00:25:27,340 --> 00:25:31,500
The truck can also facilitate communications between various agencies.

323
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:34,560
There's different types of radios in layman's terms.

324
00:25:34,561 --> 00:25:40,200
they operate on different frequencies, so people on UHF can't talk to people on 800 megahertz radios.

325
00:25:40,680 --> 00:25:46,220
And what interoperability systems do, it enables these different radio frequency types

326
00:25:46,221 --> 00:25:53,460
to talk to the vehicle, and the vehicle takes and puts them all into basically the same communication system.

327
00:25:55,320 --> 00:25:59,660
In the field, life or death can depend on everything working properly.

328
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:04,480
Back in Baltimore, the Mobile Command Center performed just as it was designed to,

329
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:09,560
This truck is a prototype that's been a labor of love put together.

330
00:26:09,561 --> 00:26:12,560
It's a working man's truck. This is cutting-edge technology.

331
00:26:12,561 --> 00:26:16,560
I'm glad the truck was here. Job well done. Absolutely.

332
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:27,560
LDV installed more than 14,000 pounds of special equipment in the Baltimore Mobile Command Center.

333
00:26:27,561 --> 00:26:34,440
center extreme trucks will return on modern marvels here on the history channel

334
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:52,200
welcome to the world of concrete the industry's largest annual event

335
00:26:52,201 --> 00:27:01,200
where manufacturers demonstrate the latest products, tools, and applications for...

336
00:27:02,080 --> 00:27:03,100
You got it.

337
00:27:03,600 --> 00:27:04,300
Concrete.

338
00:27:06,880 --> 00:27:10,540
Globally, 5 billion cubic yards of this stuff are produced each year.

339
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:13,200
That's enough concrete to...

340
00:27:13,900 --> 00:27:15,440
Well, it's a lot of concrete.

341
00:27:16,700 --> 00:27:19,580
Some of the biggest and most impressive products at the show

342
00:27:19,581 --> 00:27:23,180
are the extreme trucks used to transport and place concrete.

343
00:27:27,980 --> 00:27:32,120
Mack Trucks, a name synonymous with big vehicles for the past hundred years,

344
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:36,920
is one of the largest chassis manufacturers of ready-mix concrete trucks today.

345
00:27:39,820 --> 00:27:45,220
When building a ready-mix truck, the most important thing to keep in mind is that it has to be lightweight.

346
00:27:45,820 --> 00:27:48,520
The lighter the truck, the more weight you can carry.

347
00:27:48,521 --> 00:27:52,300
and ready-mix concrete weighs about 4,000 pounds a yard.

348
00:27:52,780 --> 00:27:56,680
And the business survives on delivering as many yards of concrete as possible.

349
00:27:57,920 --> 00:28:01,360
Most concrete isn't mixed on site for economic reasons.

350
00:28:01,800 --> 00:28:06,300
When it's delivering ready-mix, the barrel has to always be turning, keeping it from hardening it.

351
00:28:07,160 --> 00:28:11,720
We use a rear-engine PTO connected to the engine, which is driving a pump system.

352
00:28:12,120 --> 00:28:16,100
And the pump is going through a transmission, which is turning the ready-mix barrel.

353
00:28:16,101 --> 00:28:23,360
Inside that barrel there's fins and as the barrel rotates those fins mix the

354
00:28:23,361 --> 00:28:27,340
aggregate and the cement powder and the ash.

355
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:33,800
Once at the job site the mixer truck changes the rotation of the barrel and

356
00:28:33,801 --> 00:28:40,340
out spurts 10 cubic yards of concrete. The fins inside now act like a corkscrew

357
00:28:40,341 --> 00:28:46,080
in reverse. This truck will discharge ready mix in about 60 seconds.

358
00:28:46,081 --> 00:28:52,420
just very quick. When finished the drum is washed out with water to prevent

359
00:28:52,421 --> 00:29:00,920
build-up. These trucks make it look easy but that hasn't always been the case.

360
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:08,600
All concrete used to be mixed on site but the sand, rock, water and cement took up

361
00:29:08,601 --> 00:29:15,520
a lot of space. To save space ready-mix plants started appearing around 1910.

362
00:29:15,521 --> 00:29:22,520
Concrete was transported in open dump trucks.

363
00:29:22,521 --> 00:29:29,520
Unfortunately, if the trucks were delayed for any reason, the concrete would start to set up, ruining the batch.

364
00:29:29,521 --> 00:29:32,520
And perhaps the truck as well.

365
00:29:32,521 --> 00:29:40,520
Then in 1916, Stephen Stepanian of Columbus, Ohio, turned concrete transportation on its head

366
00:29:40,521 --> 00:29:44,520
when he applied for a patent for the first rotating drum truck mixer.

367
00:29:47,220 --> 00:29:52,820
Early mixers like Stefanian's design used a revolving cylinder with fins on the inside to mix the batch.

368
00:29:56,020 --> 00:30:00,520
Then in 1937, Smith Mixers of Milwaukee revolutionized the business

369
00:30:01,220 --> 00:30:05,520
when they introduced the inclined axis drum mixer, similar to today's mixers.

370
00:30:05,521 --> 00:30:09,600
Its main benefit was a higher discharge height.

371
00:30:10,860 --> 00:30:15,160
The higher the concrete is discharged, the farther from the truck it will flow,

372
00:30:15,740 --> 00:30:17,340
saving a lot of manual carting.

373
00:30:18,360 --> 00:30:23,680
Over the last 60 years, the introduction of more powerful diesel engines and air brakes

374
00:30:23,681 --> 00:30:29,800
enabled trucks to grow bigger, and they can now handle a 40,000-pound payload, or more.

375
00:30:31,040 --> 00:30:34,400
Today's mixers are often used to feed even more extreme trucks.

376
00:30:34,401 --> 00:30:36,300
concrete pumpers.

377
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:41,680
A concrete pumper basically is a telescoping boom

378
00:30:41,681 --> 00:30:43,300
mounted on a chassis,

379
00:30:43,640 --> 00:30:47,320
and it takes concrete from a cement mixer

380
00:30:47,321 --> 00:30:49,840
and pumps it to that location.

381
00:30:52,120 --> 00:30:55,440
A pumper is used to precisely place concrete.

382
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:03,700
Mixers unload into the hopper of the pumper.

383
00:31:04,400 --> 00:31:11,400
A pumping mechanism forces concrete through pipes which are connected to the boom.

384
00:31:11,401 --> 00:31:18,400
At the end of the boom, there's a length of hose to get concrete exactly where it's wanted.

385
00:31:18,401 --> 00:31:28,400
An operator controls the flow of concrete and positioning of the boom with a remote control.

386
00:31:28,401 --> 00:31:33,400
These booms can have an enormous reach.

387
00:31:33,401 --> 00:31:40,080
Today's longest boom stretch nearly 200 feet. That means a pumper could be parked

388
00:31:40,081 --> 00:31:48,860
in front of your house, reach over it, and pour a pool in your backyard.

389
00:31:48,861 --> 00:32:02,861
The Mack Mod Center in Mukunji, Pennsylvania, builds chassis for various manufacturers of

390
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:06,320
enormous pumpers.

391
00:32:06,321 --> 00:32:14,020
The Mod Center is where people come for special orders.

392
00:32:14,021 --> 00:32:16,860
This chassis is for Schwing America Incorporated.

393
00:32:16,861 --> 00:32:22,100
They rate them by the length that the boom can reach and this particular unit

394
00:32:22,101 --> 00:32:28,800
will be for a 61 meter meaning it can go from ground floor up 61 meters so if

395
00:32:28,801 --> 00:32:33,620
you were at the end zone on the football field you could reach out almost two

396
00:32:33,621 --> 00:32:39,520
thirds the length of that football field and lay concrete. To accommodate the boom

397
00:32:39,521 --> 00:32:44,180
the mod center is adding 15 feet of length and several axles to a standard

398
00:32:44,181 --> 00:32:48,887
truck. As you can see this is an extremely long truck.

399
00:32:48,888 --> 00:32:52,200
As a matter of fact it has the longest

400
00:32:52,201 --> 00:32:55,194
frame that we can physically manufacture today.

401
00:32:55,195 --> 00:32:58,380
It is 40 feet long in the basic frame. And on this

402
00:32:58,381 --> 00:33:00,612
chassis we have three steer axles.

403
00:33:00,613 --> 00:33:04,420
The three steer axles are all designed to turn a different

404
00:33:04,421 --> 00:33:05,121
amount.

405
00:33:05,122 --> 00:33:09,900
Each the front turns more than the second, the second turns more than the third, so that all

406
00:33:09,901 --> 00:33:14,640
three axles will help the vehicle make it around the curve without sliding or scrubbing.

407
00:33:17,100 --> 00:33:21,720
In the rear, we have three drive axles. Where a normal truck would have two,

408
00:33:22,260 --> 00:33:24,090
this one requires three.

409
00:33:24,091 --> 00:33:29,580
The way we accomplish that, if this would be the two normal drive axles,

410
00:33:29,581 --> 00:33:35,580
we had an additional one in front of it, which is basically a dupe of this axle and drive train

411
00:33:35,581 --> 00:33:37,180
up here as well.

412
00:33:39,100 --> 00:33:42,640
The seventh axle is a liftable, steerable tag,

413
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:45,100
which is lowered for highway travel

414
00:33:45,101 --> 00:33:48,220
and usually raised once at the work site.

415
00:33:49,980 --> 00:33:53,700
The truck has more axles to distribute the pumpers' enormous weight.

416
00:33:55,060 --> 00:33:58,100
So if you're keeping track, that's 20 tires.

417
00:33:59,160 --> 00:34:03,900
A completed vehicle with boom will weigh around 110,000 pounds.

418
00:34:05,580 --> 00:34:08,660
50% heavier than an average loaded semi.

419
00:34:10,960 --> 00:34:13,360
When this particular Mack chassis is finished,

420
00:34:13,760 --> 00:34:16,460
it's driven to the Schwing factory in White Bear, Minnesota,

421
00:34:17,260 --> 00:34:22,400
where the steel booms and pumper mechanisms are attached.

422
00:34:27,460 --> 00:34:30,240
They also install four giant legs.

423
00:34:31,100 --> 00:34:34,740
There's four outriggers, they're called,

424
00:34:34,741 --> 00:34:39,200
and in this particular unit, the two in the front will telescope out,

425
00:34:39,860 --> 00:34:42,720
and then they have a piston that goes down.

426
00:34:43,160 --> 00:34:50,120
They put a large pad on the ground surface to ensure that the unit is perfectly level in all directions,

427
00:34:50,320 --> 00:34:51,760
fore and aft and side to side.

428
00:34:53,900 --> 00:35:00,400
At the job site, pumper trucks are double teamed by mixers, unloading one every two to three minutes.

429
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:06,320
One pumper can place 3,200 tons of concrete per eight-hour day.

430
00:35:07,180 --> 00:35:13,920
But it's just another day at work for these trucks which really pour it on and pump it up.

431
00:35:18,020 --> 00:35:25,460
The paint on the 61-meter Schwing concrete pumper adds an additional 600 pounds to the weight of the truck.

432
00:35:26,860 --> 00:35:29,740
Extreme Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

433
00:35:30,400 --> 00:35:34,560
Galena, Kansas.

434
00:35:36,320 --> 00:35:38,300
A small town in the Midwest.

435
00:35:39,500 --> 00:35:42,360
A friendly place with a population of 2,500.

436
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:46,180
And perhaps a slightly slower pace of life.

437
00:35:46,960 --> 00:35:49,180
With one major exception.

438
00:35:51,980 --> 00:35:56,620
Galena is the home base of Les Shockley and his two jet trucks.

439
00:35:56,621 --> 00:36:04,580
Shockwave, a 1984 Peterbilt semi, and Super Shockwave, a 1957 Chevy pickup.

440
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:10,160
Screaming down the track at over 300 miles per hour,

441
00:36:11,260 --> 00:36:16,420
the trucks thrill fans at air shows and drag racing competitions throughout the United States.

442
00:36:16,900 --> 00:36:21,460
My father always described it as being shot out of a cannon, but I've never been shot out of a cannon.

443
00:36:26,620 --> 00:36:30,980
By the end of the run, it's pulling 6.5 Gs.

444
00:36:32,140 --> 00:36:38,220
And when we pull a chute, we're experiencing up to 10 Gs negative.

445
00:36:39,160 --> 00:36:41,500
13 Gs, they tell me, will detach your retinas.

446
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:47,540
It certainly has spectators' eyes popping out.

447
00:36:56,620 --> 00:37:05,320
Not too many trucks race an aircraft or dogfighter stunt planes.

448
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:09,040
You want something that people, when they leave, they're just not going,

449
00:37:09,140 --> 00:37:12,780
that's all right. You want them to go, oh, my God, and they do, too.

450
00:37:20,360 --> 00:37:23,520
Racing the Shockwave jet trucks is a real family affair.

451
00:37:23,521 --> 00:37:30,360
Les Shockley is the face behind the fireball.

452
00:37:31,620 --> 00:37:35,580
After years of racing both conventional race cars and jet dragsters,

453
00:37:36,200 --> 00:37:42,860
he designed and built his first jet truck, Shockwave, in 1984.

454
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:50,380
In 1996, he turned over full-time racing to his two sons, Kent and Scott.

455
00:37:50,381 --> 00:37:55,580
today Kent's back with his father at the Shockley shop in Galena

456
00:37:55,581 --> 00:37:59,140
to make adjustments for his next show

457
00:37:59,141 --> 00:38:02,940
meanwhile Scott's in Mount Vernon Illinois

458
00:38:02,941 --> 00:38:05,380
for the weekend's air show spectacular

459
00:38:05,381 --> 00:38:13,920
this truck has two after burning

460
00:38:13,921 --> 00:38:17,160
J34-48 jet engines here

461
00:38:17,161 --> 00:38:22,960
that we fully modify that come off a Navy T-2A Buckeye jet fighter trainer.

462
00:38:23,580 --> 00:38:26,420
The airplane just had a single engine, non-afterburning,

463
00:38:26,980 --> 00:38:30,160
and then from right here back is all afterburner.

464
00:38:30,260 --> 00:38:31,620
It makes all the power to the truck.

465
00:38:33,160 --> 00:38:37,940
The 57's two jet engines produce 25,000 horsepower.

466
00:38:37,941 --> 00:38:48,220
The Peterbilt has three jet engines, producing 36,000 horsepower.

467
00:38:50,040 --> 00:38:54,220
That's like the combined horsepower of 48 NASCARs.

468
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:05,260
The Shockleys have been racing jet vehicles since the mid-1970s.

469
00:39:05,261 --> 00:39:10,760
But the first jet dragster dates back another 15 years.

470
00:39:11,600 --> 00:39:18,360
In 1960, when Walt Arfons fit a Westinghouse J46 jet engine to the back of a dragster,

471
00:39:19,160 --> 00:39:21,840
he created the Green Monster No. 16.

472
00:39:23,600 --> 00:39:27,960
The car was described as a big cart with a jet engine bolted onto its rails.

473
00:39:29,440 --> 00:39:35,160
By adding an afterburner, he was able to push the car above 200 miles per hour in the quarter mile.

474
00:39:35,260 --> 00:39:40,020
making it the fastest and only jet car in the world.

475
00:39:41,240 --> 00:39:44,220
But by the mid-60s, he had company.

476
00:39:45,460 --> 00:39:47,780
Several jet dragsters started making the rounds.

477
00:39:49,560 --> 00:39:55,440
One of those cars made quite a splash with a 16-year-old Les Shockley and his future wife, Donna.

478
00:39:56,520 --> 00:40:00,160
This guy pulled it to the starting line and revved this thing up,

479
00:40:00,300 --> 00:40:02,840
and when he hit the afterburner on this thing,

480
00:40:02,841 --> 00:40:09,640
My wife had a snow cone in her hand, slung it over her shoulder right into the face of the guy standing right behind her.

481
00:40:10,460 --> 00:40:16,880
And I thought, now those things have a lot of potential because everybody was just totally blown away by it.

482
00:40:18,660 --> 00:40:23,780
Jet car races and exhibitions continue to blow fans away through the 1960s.

483
00:40:26,100 --> 00:40:32,580
And in 1972, Les finally persuaded his wife Donna to let him build a jet dragster of his own.

484
00:40:32,840 --> 00:40:37,180
After winning three national jet car championships,

485
00:40:39,720 --> 00:40:45,000
Les' oldest son, Kent, suggested turning a full-sized semi into a jet truck.

486
00:40:46,600 --> 00:40:50,640
However, at the time, there were other jet trucks racing with single engines,

487
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:52,880
not nearly good enough for Les.

488
00:40:54,360 --> 00:40:59,120
We had the two bottom engines sitting in it, and I stood back and I looked at it.

489
00:40:59,121 --> 00:41:04,280
The engines barely stick above the tires, and it didn't even look like a jet truck to me.

490
00:41:04,281 --> 00:41:11,000
I came home from work one day, and Dad had a third engine hoisted hanging from the rafters in the shop.

491
00:41:11,120 --> 00:41:18,400
So I picked it up with a hoist and rolled the truck under it, and I set it up on top, and I looked at it, and I said, no, that looks like a jet truck.

492
00:41:18,660 --> 00:41:20,711
I was like, oh, man, you know, two was nuts.

493
00:41:20,712 --> 00:41:24,440
Dad really went crazy, and everybody thought, man, Dad just went nuts, you know.

494
00:41:24,860 --> 00:41:27,420
Two's nuts, you know. Why would you put three on there?

495
00:41:27,540 --> 00:41:28,240
Why not?

496
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:43,120
In 1991, those three engines helped Les reach 376 miles per hour to set a Guinness record for a full-size truck.

497
00:41:45,960 --> 00:41:50,840
Basically, the steering and the brakes of this vehicle operate like an automobile.

498
00:41:51,360 --> 00:41:54,360
Everything else about operating a vehicle is like an airplane.

499
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:57,480
We use hand controls just like an aircraft.

500
00:41:57,481 --> 00:41:59,220
This deploys the parachute.

501
00:41:59,980 --> 00:42:03,060
This is our AB fuel control here, this lever here.

502
00:42:03,580 --> 00:42:04,940
Forward, the fuel's on.

503
00:42:05,280 --> 00:42:06,600
Backwards, the fuel's off.

504
00:42:07,420 --> 00:42:08,800
These are the three throttles.

505
00:42:09,240 --> 00:42:11,040
We have a throttle for each engine.

506
00:42:16,120 --> 00:42:20,740
In 1993, the Shockleys decided to build a second jet truck.

507
00:42:21,400 --> 00:42:24,000
This time they decided to do something a little retro.

508
00:42:24,800 --> 00:42:26,780
A 57 Chevy truck.

509
00:42:27,480 --> 00:42:31,940
The fuel we're actually using this truck is over-the-road diesel.

510
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:35,140
Got two saddle tanks, one on each side.

511
00:42:35,300 --> 00:42:37,260
Each one holds 40 gallons of diesel.

512
00:42:37,840 --> 00:42:39,480
This baby sucks the fuel down.

513
00:42:43,600 --> 00:42:48,540
The twin-engine Beast swallows 30 gallons of diesel on a 12-second run.

514
00:42:51,820 --> 00:42:54,140
Getting the truck to go fast isn't a problem.

515
00:42:55,100 --> 00:42:56,880
It's keeping it on the ground that's tricky.

516
00:42:57,480 --> 00:43:00,940
The wings, the wings up above, got one back here.

517
00:43:01,640 --> 00:43:07,420
This produces at 300 miles an hour, it produces 3,500 pounds of downforce on the truck.

518
00:43:07,620 --> 00:43:15,000
And the one up here, right over the cab, we figure produces 5,000 pounds of downforce on the truck.

519
00:43:15,280 --> 00:43:19,620
So if you took these wings off the truck, she would actually try to start flying.

520
00:43:20,260 --> 00:43:23,180
Put the wings on it, it's on the ground, and that's where we like to stay.

521
00:43:23,181 --> 00:43:29,020
when Les set the Guinness record of 376 miles per hour

522
00:43:29,021 --> 00:43:31,680
the truck's front end came off the ground

523
00:43:31,681 --> 00:43:33,800
and the tires started coming apart

524
00:43:33,801 --> 00:43:40,180
but just how fast can these jet diesels go?

525
00:43:40,660 --> 00:43:44,540
this will run over 400 but dad just don't want us to do it right now

526
00:43:44,541 --> 00:43:47,380
I think he just wants to hold on the record a little bit longer

527
00:43:47,381 --> 00:43:49,280
but I'm going after it someday

528
00:43:49,281 --> 00:43:59,740
shockwave might be the fastest in our pantheon of extreme trucks

529
00:43:59,741 --> 00:44:07,820
but the others hold their own in the mud or on the site collecting or protecting

530
00:44:07,821 --> 00:44:15,220
they all have an extreme job to do even when that job is all about getting fired

